ath12k: ipq5332: put the board data at the base of its region

ath12k places the board data at reserved_mem[0].base plus
hw_params->bdf_addr_offset, which is 0xC00000 for IPQ5332. On the Xiaomi
BE3600 Pro that is 0x4c900000, in the last megabyte of q6_ipq5332_data.

The vendor device tree puts it at 0x4bd00000 -- the base of that same
region -- for every entry of its qcom,bdf-addr array, and the M3 image
links at 0x4bd40000, exactly 256 KiB later. The two abut with nothing
between them, which is what a 256 KiB board data region at the base
produces.

This firmware is already known to use its own address for the calibration
database rather than the one QMI hands it, so the same is plausible for
the board data.

Signed-off-by: Hideo Sumi <hideo.sumi@mugops.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/hw.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/hw.c
@@ -1649,7 +1649,23 @@
 
 		.ce_ie_addr = &ath12k_ce_ie_addr_ipq5332,
 		.ce_remap = &ath12k_ce_remap_ipq5332,
-		.bdf_addr_offset = 0xC00000,
+
+		/* The board data goes at the base of the reserved region, not
+		 * 12 MiB into it.
+		 *
+		 * The vendor device tree for this SoC puts it at 0x4bd00000 for
+		 * every memory mode, which is q6_ipq5332_data's base, and its M3
+		 * image links at 0x4bd40000 -- exactly 256 KiB later, so the two
+		 * abut with nothing between them. An offset of 0xC00000 instead
+		 * lands the board data at 0x4c900000, in the last megabyte of the
+		 * window.
+		 *
+		 * This firmware is already known to use its own address for the
+		 * calibration database rather than the one QMI hands it, so it
+		 * plausibly does the same for the board data, in which case it
+		 * reads whatever happens to be at 0x4bd00000.
+		 */
+		.bdf_addr_offset = 0,
 
 		.dp_primary_link_only = true,
 	},
